{#
    One purchase, in full, with the decision on it (FR-094, FR-095, FR-099, NFR-094).

    **Two layouts, one page.** A child may *view* the purchase they asked for — FR-095 requires
    them to see it change from Pending to Confirmed, and the parent's note with it — but the
    family shell's navigation is a parent's and every link in it would answer 403 for them
    (FR-068). So the viewer who cannot decide gets the player shell. Whether they can decide is
    the voter's answer, computed in the controller, never a guess from a role here.

    **One form, two buttons, two routes.** FR-094 puts Approve and Deny on separate endpoints and
    lets the parent attach a note to either. The Deny button carries `formaction`, which is plain
    HTML: it works with JavaScript off, both submits carry the same CSRF token, and the note the
    parent typed goes with whichever they press. Two forms would give them two note boxes, one of
    which would silently throw away what they wrote.

    **Approve and Deny are distinct, clearly labelled actions and never colour-only** (NFR-094).
    Each has its own words, each names the child and the purchase in its accessible name, and
    Deny is additionally confirmed — by `confirm-dialog.js` when scripting is available, and not
    at all when it is not, because the confirmation is a courtesy and the server is the boundary.

    **"Request more info" is not here** (G-31): the spec lists it and defines no recipient, no
    channel, no resulting state and no effect on the 48-hour clock. The note below says so rather
    than leaving a parent to wonder where the third button went.
#}
{% extends canDecide ? 'family/_layout.html.twig' : 'player/_layout.html.twig' %}
{% form_theme form 'account/_form_theme.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ purchase.purchaseDescription }}{% endblock %}
{% block heading %}{{ purchase.purchaseDescription }}{% endblock %}

{% block family_content %}{{ block('approval_body') }}{% endblock %}
{% block player_content %}{{ block('approval_body') }}{% endblock %}

{% block approval_body %}
    <article class="purchase-card status-{{ purchase.status.value }}">
        <div class="headline">
            <h2>{{ purchase.childProfile.displayName }}</h2>
            {{ include('approval/_amount.html.twig', {amount: purchase.amount}) }}
        </div>

        <p class="meta">
            <span class="status status-{{ purchase.status.value }}">{{ purchase.status.label }}</span>
            <span>Paid with {{ purchase.paymentType.label|lower }}</span>
            <span>Asked {{ purchase.requestedAt|date('j M Y, H:i') }}</span>
            {% if purchase.respondedAt %}
                <span>Answered {{ purchase.respondedAt|date('j M Y, H:i') }}</span>
            {% endif %}
        </p>

        <p>{{ purchase.status.explanation }}</p>

        {% if purchase.status.isPending %}
            {{ include('approval/_countdown.html.twig', {expiresAt: purchase.expiresAt}) }}
        {% endif %}

        {% if purchase.parentNotes %}
            <p class="notes"><strong>Note:</strong> {{ purchase.parentNotes }}</p>
        {% endif %}

        {% if purchase.paymentReference %}
            {# Shown because it is the proof the payment happened exactly once, and because the
               stand-in processor's references are visibly fake — nobody should mistake this
               environment for one that took money (FR-097, D-04). #}
            <p class="muted">Payment reference: {{ purchase.paymentReference }}</p>
        {% endif %}
    </article>

    {% if canDecide and purchase.status.isPending %}
        {{ form_start(form) }}
            {{ include('account/_error_summary.html.twig', {form: form}) }}

            {{ form_row(form.notes) }}

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit" name="decision" value="approve">
                    <span aria-hidden="true">Approve</span>
                    <span class="visually-hidden">
                        Approve {{ purchase.childProfile.displayName }}'s purchase of {{ purchase.purchaseDescription }} for {{ purchase.amount.spokenLabel }}
                    </span>
                </button>

                <button
                    type="submit"
                    name="decision"
                    value="deny"
                    formaction="{{ path('family_approval_deny', {id: purchase.id}) }}"
                    data-confirm-action="Deny this purchase? {{ purchase.childProfile.displayName }} will be told, and nothing will be charged."
                    data-confirm-label="Deny"
                >
                    <span aria-hidden="true">Deny</span>
                    <span class="visually-hidden">
                        Deny {{ purchase.childProfile.displayName }}'s purchase of {{ purchase.purchaseDescription }}
                    </span>
                </button>

                <a href="{{ path('family_approvals') }}">Back to approvals</a>
            </div>
        {{ form_end(form) }}

        <p class="muted">
            There is no "request more info" here yet: the specification asks for it without saying
            who would receive it, how they would answer, or whether the 48-hour clock would keep
            running. Ask your child directly for now.
        </p>
    {% elseif canDecide %}
        <p><a href="{{ path('family_approvals') }}">Back to approvals</a></p>
    {% else %}
        <p><a href="{{ path('player_reservations') }}">Back to my reservations</a></p>
    {% endif %}

    <style>{{ include('approval/_status_styles.html.twig') }}</style>
{% endblock %}
